# The second input behind the reporter suites, and the only way to see two checks fail:
# "Ontology without declaration" and "Ontology without description". reporters.ttl cannot
# show them, because it declares a described ontology and a file cannot do both.
#
# It is a fragment — a file of terms with no owl:Ontology header of its own. ontolint's
# rule is one file, one ontology, so this is reported rather than tolerated. Linted beside
# reporters.ttl it also gives the three-way split the declaration check actually has: none
# of the files declared one, some of them did, all of them did.
#
# Every term here fails one check, as in reporters.ttl. What this file cannot avoid is the
# other direction: two checks report terms it never put under test. With no declared
# namespace to compare against, undefined-terms reports its own three terms and
# untyped-property reports rdf:type, rdfs:label and friends. Those are false positives
# rather than fixture content — that is #102, and pinning it is part of what this file is
# for.
@prefix frag: <https://example.org/fragment#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

frag:Tool a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Tool" ;
    rdfs:comment "Something used to do work." .

frag:Worker a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Worker" ;
    rdfs:comment "Someone who uses a tool." .

frag:usedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "used by" ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a tool to the worker who uses it." ;
    rdfs:domain frag:Tool ;
    rdfs:range frag:Worker .
